          "secNumber": "6.2",
          "heading": "Evidence and responsible interpretation",
          "content": "An immune response depends on anatomical compartment and previous exposure. Blood samples provide valuable, accessible information but can miss tissue-resident populations and localized mediator gradients. Bulk averages hide rare cell populations and interactions. Healthy versus diseased comparisons can confound cause and consequence, while an intervention that changes two pathways at once may not isolate either one. For quantitative work, preserve raw counts, clarify calibration, distinguish independent donors from repeated technical wells, and describe measurement error separately from biological variation.\n\nAll activities in this unit are computational, synthetic-data or abstract analysis exercises. Do not culture pathogens, perform human or animal exposure experiments, or attempt clinical assays on personal samples from these pages. Appropriate institutional biosafety and ethics review are prerequisites for any real laboratory research. These pages never prescribe immunization, diagnosis or treatment.",
